    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Dengue virus (DENV) is a human pathogen that represents a major global health threat, yet there are no approved antivirals or vaccines available. The traditional approach in antiviral development has been to utilize small molecules to inhibit specific viral enzymatic functions. However, the concept of inhibiting the virus by targeting host proteins involved in the viral life cycle has not been fully explored. As such, there is a need to identify and characterize host proteins that may be involved in processes such as viral RNA replication and assembly. To this end, we pursued two parallel bioinformatics approaches developed by the Khatri lab to identify, upregulated genes in DENV infection and candidate drugs targeting host factors with predicted antiviral effects against DENV. GOSR2, a host cell golgi SNAP receptor, was identified using the first approach, however, we were unable to achieve a robust defect in DENV infection upon knockdown of this gene or overexpression of a dominant negative mutant. Using the second approach, we identified two drugs, obatoclax and perphenazine, which exhibited potent antiviral effects against DENV. We specifically demonstrate that obatoclax inhibits DENV entry and RNA replication, while perphenazine inhibits DENV RNA replication and release. More selective inhibitors against the known molecular targets of obatoclax, the pro-survival members of the Bcl2 family, were also effective in inhibiting DENV infection. This suggests that these members of the Bcl2 family are essential to the DENV life cycle and could serve as potential DENV antiviral targets. Furthermore, since multiple viruses may hijack these host proteins, such host targets could form the basis of broad-spectrum antiviral strategies to be employed against a variety of viruses, beyond DENV.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
